// g7250_control.repl — G7250 Control unit (STM32H743-class), skeleton.
//
// Clean-room: every value below is traced to the g7250-base RE docs
// (control_unit/docs/*) or to ST's public reference manual RM0433 (H742/H743),
// used as DATA. No stock firmware code. Confidence tags mirror the RE docs
// (VERIFIED/HIGH/HYPOTHESIS).
//
// Starts from Renode's own STM32H7 base and overrides the Control-specific
// facts. The image itself is loaded by the harness from the local image-map
// (never from here); this file only models the reset state the bootloader
// leaves behind (app vector table at 0x08020000).

using "platforms/cpus/stm32h7.repl"

// --- MCU identity -----------------------------------------------------------
// STM32H743-class, Cortex-M7. The single strongest pointer is the initial MSP
// 0x24080000 = top of a 512 KiB AXI SRAM (the H743/H750/H753 size), VERIFIED.
// The reset handler also runs the rev-Y AXI-SRAM errata workaround and the
// standard CMSIS SystemInit (SCB->VTOR = 0x08020000). cortex-m7 + 16 MPU
// regions come from the base.

// --- Memory map (control_unit/docs/memory_map.md, VERIFIED) -----------------
// AXI SRAM is the FULL 512 KiB 0x24000000..0x24080000 on this part, which is
// exactly the H743 base default (0x80000). So, unlike the Main unit (320 KiB
// H72x), NO axiSram override is needed here. Nothing is placed in ITCM, DTCM
// or D2 SRAM; the base sizes (DTCM 128 KiB, SRAM4 64 KiB, backup 4 KiB) all
// match the MPU regions in memory_map.md and are left as the base provides.
//
//   .data  0x24000000..0x24001698 (<- flash 0x080DD568)   VERIFIED
//   .bss   0x240016A0..0x240614A4 (zeroed)                 VERIFIED
//   heap+stack up to 0x24080000, initial MSP 0x24080000    VERIFIED
//
// Application image: 0x08020000..0x080DEC10 (781 328 B), vector table
// @0x08020000. flashBank1 (1 MiB @0x08000000) from the base covers the app +
// the absent bootloader region.

// --- SDRAM framebuffer (docs/memory_map.md + display subsystem, VERIFIED) ---
// External SDRAM on FMC bank 1 @0xC0000000, 32 MiB (9 col / 13 row bits, 4
// banks, 16-bit). The base sdramBank1 is 256 MiB; narrow it to the real 32 MiB
// so an out-of-range framebuffer access is caught rather than silently
// absorbed. Framebuffers at 0xC0000000 / 0xC012C000, emWin pool at 0xC0800000
// all fall inside. sdramBank2 (0xD0000000) is unused by the Control and left
// as the base provides.
sdramBank1:
    size: 0x2000000          // 32 MiB @0xC0000000 (VERIFIED)

// --- SPI controllers absent from the H743 base (base has only spi4) ---------
// SPI1 drives the W25Qxx SPI NOR flash (FatFS `1:` + the USB mass-storage
// LUN), master/mode-0/48 MHz, CS on PG13 (VERIFIED, docs/peripherals.md).
// SPI2 runs as I2S2, the full-duplex 48 kHz audio link to the Control AIC3204
// (VERIFIED). H7 base addresses from RM0433 (SPI1 0x40013000 APB2, SPI2
// 0x40003800 APB1). Registered without an rcc alias (the base's
// RegistrationPeripheralAlias enum only carries SPI4); clock-gating awareness
// is not needed for the boot skeleton. Verified to register on Renode.
// TODO(control): attach the W25Qxx SPI slave to spi1 and the AIC3204 I2S slave
// to spi2 once the image drives the frames; add DMA1 wiring (S0 RX / S1 TX
// circular for I2S2) if the boot uses it.
spi1: SPI.STM32H7_SPI @ sysbus 0x40013000
spi2: SPI.STM32H7_SPI @ sysbus 0x40003800

// --- Peripherals already present in the H743 base (Control inventory) -------
// The Control is a plain H743-class part, so the base already provides every
// block the boot needs: RCC (0x58024400), PWR (0x58024800, Python stub),
// FLASH (0x52002000), FMC (0x52004000, Python stub), GPIO A..K, EXTI/SYSCFG,
// NVIC, DWT, I2C1/2/3, DMA1/2, DMAMUX, TIM1..17, LTDC, DMA2D, SDMMC
// (0x52007000), USB OTG (usb2 @0x40080000 = OTG_FS), RTC, IWDG, CRC.
// Nothing H72x-only is needed (contrast g7250_main.repl's TIM23/24,
// UART9/USART10 tags): the Control part class carries all of these natively.
//
// TODO(control) device models to add in models/devices/ and wire here when the
// image drives the buses (host-testable logic first, Renode shims after):
//   - W25Qxx SPI NOR on SPI1: JEDEC-ID (0xEF4014/15/17/18), 0x03 read, 0x02
//     page-program, 0x20 sector-erase, 0x05 WIP status. FatFS `1:` + USB LUN.
//   - AIC3204 codec on I2C2 0x18 (I2S master): a Control-side peer of the
//     Main codec stub in models/devices/i2c_devices.py (different init: PLL
//     98.304 MHz, 48 kHz, codec drives BCLK/WCLK). Reset on PH2.
//   - GT911 capacitive touch on I2C1 0x14 (fallback 0x5D): 186-byte config
//     block + checksum, 42-byte report. INT on PE2 (EXTI2), RST on PG14.
//   - PCA9557 I/O expander on I2C2 0x19: all-outputs, boot value 0x8B; bits 4
//     (BT power), 5 (shutdown pulse), 6 (TX path), 7 (RX path). Reset on PG3.
//   - LTDC panel + FMC SDRAM: the base models cover register access; the
//     panel power sequence (PE6/PI3/PF9) and backlight (TIM4 PD12/PD13) are
//     GPIO/timer wiggles the base absorbs for the skeleton.
//
// TODO(control) front panel (later milestone, needs the image's scan frames):
//   - GPIO button matrix (5x5 + direct), 8 GPIO quadrature encoders, TIM2
//     main-dial encoder, H-22 mic keypad on TIM8/PI5 (ratio-coded serial).

// --- Inter-MCU link (docs/peripherals.md, VERIFIED) -------------------------
// USART2 (PD5/PD6 AF7, 460 800 8N1, DMA1 S2 TX / S3 RX circular) is the SLIP
// link to the Main USART2. Present in the base as usart2. The Control is the
// SLIP client (TIM13 50 Hz). USART1 (PA9/PA10, 115 200 8N1) is the Bluetooth
// module link; also present in the base. No peripheral to add; wiring the two
// MCUs together over SLIP is a later (multi-machine) milestone.

// --- Faults (control_unit/docs/startup.md, VERIFIED) ------------------------
// Default handler 0x08023600 (loop); NMI/MemManage/BusFault/UsageFault are bare
// loops; SVC/DebugMon/PendSV return at once. HardFault logs one line via the
// console logger and halts (no register dump). IWDG is refreshed from SysTick
// every 256 ticks, so a stuck superloop does NOT trip the watchdog by itself.
// Modelled at firmware level; the emulator just needs USART1 (the stdout
// console) to accept the log line without blocking.
